WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
- We're in quarantine,

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.590
and apparently they were in
quarantine on "Scrubs" also,

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:04.630
and I haven't watched an episode

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.790
of "Scrubs" in quite some time.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.320
Let's watch it right now.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.455
I'll explain this in a bit.

00:00:08.455 --> 00:00:09.370
Pew!

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.610
- Before I settled on the outfit

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.720
for my first date with Kylie,

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.110
I had to run it by the experts.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.710
So be honest, but not too honest, okay,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.400
cause I'm feeling a little chunky.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.740
- My man is wearing a red t-shirt

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.567
underneath a white dress shirt,

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:25.630
untucked, with a blazer on top.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.423
He's like combining three
styles, maybe four, into one.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.883
It's a solid no for me.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.043
- [Elliot] Whoa.

00:00:34.043 --> 00:00:36.910
- Short sleeve shirt under a blazer.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.260
Why?

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.093
Oh!

00:00:39.093 --> 00:00:40.530
- Happy birthday Laverne.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.250
- Thanks Carla.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.140
- Did your brother make you face cake?

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:44.973
- Sure did.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.750
- [Carla] Wow.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.070
- Think I could have a
piece of that face cake?

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.507
- I heard there's a face cake.

00:00:50.507 --> 00:00:51.510
- [Crowd] Face cake, I heard it.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.670
- I don't know what it is with hospitals,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.690
we love bringing unhealthy
treats and snacks

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.360
to our staff lounges.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.193
I don't get it.

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:02.350
Like I know we probably stress eat

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.020
more than most professions,

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.600
but the fact that we have
the unhealthiest stuff

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.630
when we're supposed to
be examples of health.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.630
We do it sometimes in good
comradery to celebrate birthdays,

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.790
but then there's like a
birthday every other day

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.630
which means Philly fluff.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.210
If you don't know what
a Philly fluff cake is,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.183
you're missing out.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.010
And you're probably healthy.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.700
- Thanks for the shirt Lonnie.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.935
- Are you doing this because I
forgot to shave this morning?

00:01:23.935 --> 00:01:26.200
- What is going on?

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.280
Why is he wearing the name tag on his nip?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.260
- So, what's wrong with this guy?

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.100
- Well let's see, fatigue, fever, malaise.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.540
Have you been to Hong Kong, sir?

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:38.800
- I don't know if this is going

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.570
in a medically accurate direction.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.040
And on top of it, who's gonna take up

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.930
a patient's chart and
just start reading it

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.090
with a random, non-hospital employee.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.580
Although maybe she works at
a hospital, I don't know.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.520
And gone are the days of the chart sitting

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.180
at the patient's bedside,
cause people walk around there,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.670
and health information
is a protected asset.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.610
- Could be SARS.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.020
- Severe acute respiratory syndrome,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.040
and now with COVID-19,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.240
we're facing SARS-COV-2.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.688
In the beginning, actually, when the

00:02:05.688 --> 00:02:08.170
International Taxonomy of Viruses,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.310
the organization that
names all the viruses,

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.960
named it SARS-COV-2, I was so confused why

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.060
they chose to go with
that instead of COVID-19,

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.540
and then it hit me, COVID-19 was actually

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.100
the name of the disease
caused by SARS-COV-2,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.327
and SARS-COV-2 actually has a meaning

00:02:23.327 --> 00:02:27.930
of SARS Coronavirus two,
because it was the second one

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.150
after the initial SARS.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.450
- [Alarm Voice] Doctor suspects SARS,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.850
that's cause for immediate
quarantine lock down.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.960
- But why did he suspect SARS?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.930
I mean, the patient had what?

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.380
Fatigue, malaise,
basically not feeling good,

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.550
and like a fever or something?

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:45.550
That fits 10,000 if not
100,000 different illnesses.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.620
And FYI, when we do
isolations or quarantines,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.730
that's not how we do them.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.330
We don't have metal doors dropping

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.100
and alarms ringing like that,

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.790
it's a lot more controlled,

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:57.050
it's a lot smarter,

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.100
because different illnesses require

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.420
different types of precautions.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.800
Initially with COVID-19,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.970
we were doing just droplet
and contact precaution,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.440
but then as we became worried
about aerosolized particles,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.820
some hospitals went to
airborne precautions,

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.710
that required a negative
pressure air room for patients

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.250
who were admitted with the illness.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.040
That's why it was very difficult

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.160
on hospitals to manage all these patients.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.750
It's a novel virus, we
were learning as we went.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.760
Like imagine, normally
we're treating patients

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.850
with already existing
knowledge of an illness.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.250
Now we were treating patients
and learning at the same time.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.984
It's not an ideal scenario
for ideal outcomes.

00:03:30.984 --> 00:03:33.401
- What have you done, newbie?

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:35.819
- Quarantinis anyone?

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:36.652
(loud laughing)

00:03:36.652 --> 00:03:38.700
- She said, "Quarantinis."

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:39.533
That's funny.

00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:41.160
- Unfortunately, by hospital policy,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.500
we have to stay locked down

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.610
until the labs come back,

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.150
and that's going to take several hours.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.130
So let's hang in there,

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.466
and not forget this is
all Dr. Dorian's fault.

00:03:48.466 --> 00:03:50.260
(soft chuckling)

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.230
- We are short handed,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.730
there are only four doctors here.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.200
- I counted more than that.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.820
- I'm talking legitimate
doctors, turtle head.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.300
Here, pee pants is a
pathologist, so he doesn't count.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.559
Johnson is a dermatologist,
which is Greek for fake doctor.

00:04:03.559 --> 00:04:05.432
(loud laughing)

00:04:05.432 --> 00:04:06.427
- Oh my God, he said "Dermatologist

00:04:06.427 --> 00:04:07.810
"is Greek for fake doctor."

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.400
I can only imagine how a
dermatologist or pathologist

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.530
feels when they're on an
airplane and someone says

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.730
is there a doctor on
board and they volunteer.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.630
There's no way they know what to do,

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.020
unless they're fresh out of residency

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.430
and they just got some training,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.450
but otherwise, like if
you're seeing skin conditions

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.150
all the time, or you're looking at slides,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.690
how do you know how to handle anaphylaxis

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.580
in the air without an EpiPen,
or an Epi auto injector?

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.060
By the way, we're gonna
have a dermatologist

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.910
on very soon, and not to critique

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.920
my horrible SPF situation here.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.020
Please don't judge me.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.410
I know it's embarrassing.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.240
I didn't apply the SPF correctly.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.940
I don't know how I did it
so poorly, but look at this.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.790
- I'm bored, I'm a surgeon
and there's no surgery.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.170
- Why do you think I brought you in here?

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:52.090
- Oh my God, that rhythm on the monitor.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.410
I don't understand,

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.970
they have all of this equipment,

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.090
they have medical aids,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.220
but what is going on on their monitors?

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.710
The rhythms that are being shown

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.640
looks like the patient is
literally coding as we speak,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:03.680
I don't understand.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.072
Just show a proper rhythm.

00:05:05.072 --> 00:05:07.390
- The only thing I detest
more than treating patients

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.490
is treating patients on an empty stomach.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.770
I'm famished.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.020
- You've been in here for eight minutes.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.970
- I haven't eaten since yesterday.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.420
I've been starving myself because

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.920
I was going to a steak house tonight.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.160
It was just gonna be me,
a 24 ounce porter house,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.560
and a fist full of blood thinners.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.240
- This is, like, the exact opposite of the

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.490
chief of family medicine
that I worked with.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.350
Well he's technically the family
medicine program director,

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:30.770
Dr. Joseph Tribuna.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:34.110
He will starve himself
for 10 days if he has to

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.260
in order to make sure his
patients are taken care of.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:39.150
I have never seen an
attending who during rounds

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.060
will not only make sure
that they're getting

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.070
the diagnosis right, their
patients taken care of,

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.110
all of that, but then also they'll,

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.070
he would literally open a
patient's food for them,

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:49.260
help them eat it.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.130
Like no job was too big or too small

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.979
to make sure that the
patient was comfortable.

00:05:53.979 --> 00:05:56.200
(soft thunder)
Whoa.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.700
- So how's it going for Kylie?

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.830
- There was nothing exciting going on.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:01.663
This sucks!

00:06:01.663 --> 00:06:02.950
I need to look like a stud,

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.210
and a doctor can't look like a stud

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.420
unless he's saving somebody's life.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.340
I need somebody to have a heart attack.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.250
- Got $20?

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.530
- Come on Steve, stay with me buddy.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.990
You're not getting this one!

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.170
- Oh my God!

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:15.480
What are you doing?

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.730
I mean, I know it's fake.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.630
But it's too fake.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.740
It's a fake within a fake.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.930
It's a show that's poorly medically acted

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.640
on top of a fake person
having a fake heart attack.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.610
Okay, there's too many fakes.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.050
- Could you make your face
a little bit redder buddy?

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.117
Come on.
- I want more money.

00:06:32.117 --> 00:06:32.950
- What?

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.470
- $50 or I tell the girl.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:35.303
- I don't have it.

00:06:35.303 --> 00:06:36.250
- Get it.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.428
- Elliot, I need another
doctor over here, stat.

00:06:38.428 --> 00:06:39.790
(chuckling)

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.957
- He said, "I need another doctor stat."

00:06:43.208 --> 00:06:44.041
- Do you have $30.

00:06:44.041 --> 00:06:45.660
- I gave you 20, I'm tapped.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.010
Ask Johnson, he's loaded.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.336
- I need a dermatologist over here, stat!

00:06:49.336 --> 00:06:52.105
(laughing loudly)

00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:54.510
- Look how these chest
compressions are so bad.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:55.633
- Time to shine.

00:06:57.207 --> 00:06:58.240
What do you need?

00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.506
Is it a rash?

00:06:59.506 --> 00:07:00.595
(coughing)
- "Is it a rash?"

00:07:00.595 --> 00:07:01.998
- Look, I need this guy
faked a heart attack.

00:07:01.998 --> 00:07:03.802
He wants $50, we only have 20.

00:07:03.802 --> 00:07:04.635
- You know, I feel like you guys

00:07:04.635 --> 00:07:06.220
just use me for my money.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.300
- You have a trust fund,
now give me the money.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.133
- Ah!

00:07:11.235 --> 00:07:12.147
(machine beeping)

00:07:12.147 --> 00:07:13.760
- Clear!

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:14.593
- I'm alive!

00:07:14.593 --> 00:07:16.960
- Stop shocking people who's hearts

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.250
are beating normally, please.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.790
This happens too often on these shows.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:21.890
At least it's comedic here.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.980
And by the way with this heart attack,

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:25.760
if someone has a heart attack,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.170
you don't just run and start
doing chest compressions

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.950
if they're breathing and have a pulse.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:34.110
The chest compressions are
for when a heart stops.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.930
Literally, so the heart's not beating,

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.960
you're doing chest compressions
to simulate a heart beat.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.430
Like, even if you do chest
compressions perfectly,

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.120
you're still only moving the blood,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.800
circulating the blood, at about 20%

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.340
of the efficiency that the
heart would do it normally.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:49.960
So no, if someone has a heart attack,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.220
you don't just start
doing chest compressions,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.820
and you certainly don't just
shock them out of no where.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.850
The main thing you wanna do when someone's

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.450
having a heart attack is
to open up their arteries

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:00.600
that are currently blocked.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.220
And there's multiple ways we do it,

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.620
stints, there's procedures, medications,

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.540
and a lot of the times,
it's very time sensitive.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:12.620
Actually there's something
know as door-to-balloon time,

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.300
which is like when you come
in with the first signs

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.730
of a heart attack, we know,

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:18.610
we have to get you in that cath lab

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.550
to open up your blocked artery,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.810
or otherwise cardiac tissue is gonna die,

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.790
and some of the damage could be permanent.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:26.890
- Seeing you in your element today,

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.580
you seem so--
- [J.D.] Here it comes.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:30.630
Sexy.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.270
- Genuine.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:35.210
- [J.D.] She would have said
sexy if I had a mustache.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.590
- You know, James lied all the time,

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.730
and I don't know, it's just nice

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:41.390
to be with a guy I can trust.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:46.130
- Trust is the foundation
of any good relationship.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:48.560
Spousal or otherwise.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.580
Work or otherwise.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:52.053
Friendship or otherwise.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.650
Doctor or patient, or otherwise.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:57.483
I don't know anymore.

00:08:57.483 --> 00:09:01.420
- Laverne, I bet you're
tempted to break open

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:04.390
that face cake and just tear it apart.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.280
- No one's touching this.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.830
- Oh I don't care, I'm not even hungry.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.835
- Why is it called face cake?

00:09:10.835 --> 00:09:12.381
Why is it called

00:09:12.381 --> 00:09:15.714
(slow audio) face cake?

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.200
Someone explain it to me.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.033
- Hi Glen.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.620
- [Both] Hey.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.845
- I'm Glen.
- I'm Glen.

00:09:24.845 --> 00:09:27.650
Look, let's not go down that road again.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.693
- Why is his ID like
floating on his chest?

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:32.816
Do you guys see that?

00:09:32.816 --> 00:09:33.649
- I don't understand why Carla

00:09:33.649 --> 00:09:35.360
didn't tell me about you guys?

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.380
- Is it just me or in
"Scrubs" they always have

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.050
conversations over
patients that are comatose?

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.420
Just go into a meeting room.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:42.610
There's so many empty rooms.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.750
Why do you have to do it
over a patient's body?

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.720
- The lab results are
back and there is no SARS.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.030
